\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSPPL (Persistent Property List) class allows you to incrementally store property lists to and retrieve them from disk. A \i property list\i0  organizes data into named values and lists of values using four classes: NSDictionary, NSString, NSData, and NSArray. A property list is an abstract concept whose implementation can vary depending on context. For example, while NSPPL stores property lists in a binary format, in other parts of NEXTSTEP (such as the defaults system) property lists are represented in an ASCII format. NSPPL is only one mechanism for creating and storing property lists; for a discussion of other approaches, see the section \'aaAlternatives to NSPPL.\'ba\
\fs14\
\fs28 The four classes used in property lists give you the means to produce data that is meaningfully structured, transportable, storable, and accessible, but still as lightweight as possible. You represent basic data types (such as integers and text) with NSString, and binary data with NSData. You use NSDictionary and NSArray to build complex data structures. In an NSDictionary, data is structured as key-value pairs, where the key is a string, and the value can be an NSString, an NSArray, an NSData, or another NSDictionary. In an NSArray, data is structured as a collection of objects that can be accessed by index. An NSArray in an NSPPL can contain NSStrings, NSDatas, NSDictionaries, and other NSArrays. \
\fs14\
\fs28 For example, this code excerpt creates an NSPPL, \b PersonalInfo.ppl\b0 , that stores a person\'27s name, address, and children\'27s names. The example uses combinations of NSString, NSArray, and NSDictionary objects. All data is accessed through a root NSDictionary in which the person\'27s name is an NSString, the children\'27s names are stored in an NSArray, and the address is stored in an NSDictionary containing NSStrings.\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 NSPPL *ppl;\
NSString *pplPath;\tab \
NSMutableDictionary *root;\
NSString *name = @"Chris Smith";\
NSArray *childArray;\
NSMutableDictionary *addressDict;\
\
childArray = [NSArray arrayWithObjects:@"Sam", @"Bettina",\
    @"Eloise", nil];\
\
addressDict = [NSMutableDictionary dictionaryWithCapacity:4];\
/* Add data to addressDict. */\
[addressDict setObject:@"955 Elm Street" forKey:@"street"];\
[addressDict setObject:@"Midland" forKey:@"city"];\
[addressDict setObject:@"Kansas" forKey:@"state"];\
[addressDict setObject:@"19067" forKey:@"zipcode"];\
\tab \
pplPath = @"PersonalInfo.ppl";\
/* Read the NSPPL; if it doesn\'27t exist, create it. */\
ppl = [NSPPL pplWithPath:pplPath create:YES readOnly:NO];\
if (!ppl) \{\
    NSLog(@"Couldn't open or create %@", pplPath);\
    exit(1);\
 \}\
\
/* Get ppl\'27s root dictionary. */\tab \
root = [ppl rootDictionary];\
/* Through the root dictionary, add data to ppl. */\
[root setObject:name forKey:@"name"];\
[root setObject:addressDict forKey:@"address"];\
[root setObject:childArray forKey:@"children"];\
/* Save ppl. */\
[ppl save];\tab \tab \tab   \
\fs40\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Structuring a Persistent Property List\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 As the above code excerpt illustrates, an NSPPL maintains a root dictionary that\'27s used as the point of departure for whatever other objects or data structures you attach to it. How you structure your data within that dictionary is a matter of personal preference. For example, you can set up an NSDictionary of NSDictionaries, an NSDictionary containing NSArrays, and so on. The solution you choose is dependent only on what works best for your data. \
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql How Persistent Property Lists Work\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 When you instantiate an NSPPL object, a directory is created that has the name you specify. This directory contains two files: \b store\b0  and \b log\b0 . \b store\b0  and \b log\b0  work in conjunction to ensure that any changes you make to your NSPPL can be rolled back in the case of a failure. While you\'27re modifying a persistent property list, the changes are recorded in \b store\b0  and added to \b log\b0 . \b log\b0  maintains the property list\'27s old state so that if a failure occurs, the changes made in \b store\b0  can be rolled back. \
\fs14\
\fs28 Persistent property lists are atomic, meaning that if a save operation fails, the NSPPL reverts to its previously saved state. An NSPPL is never left in an intermediate state. Changes to an NSPPL are applied incrementally (in memory, but not to disk) as you make them and are reflected in the \b store\b0  and \b log\b0  files. A \b save\b0  operation has the effect of committing the changes you\'27ve made to disk.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If an NSPPL becomes damaged (for example, if there\'27s a bad bit on the disk), attempting to access it raises the exception NSInternalInconsistencyException. If this happens, you can retrieve the NSPPL by catching the exception and using the NSException \b userInfo\b0  method to get the userInfo NSDictionary. The userInfo NSDictionary usually has the NSPPL available under the key \'aaNSPPL\'ba, which you can use to rebuild your persistent property list (when possible).\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Alternatives to NSPPL\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 You can store a property list in three different ways: as an ASCII file, in a serialized binary format, and as a persistent property list (NSPPL). Each of these approaches has its advantages. For example, an ASCII property list is human-readable, but access is slow. Serialization, which stores property lists in a binary format, offers faster access than an ASCII property list and it\'27s also \i lazy\i0 , meaning that you can read parts of files without accessing the whole thing. But serialization doesn\'27t allow you to modify your data and then only re-serialize the part that changed.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Like serialization, a persistent property list stores data in a binary format, provides fast access, and is lazy. It also allows you to make incremental changes to an NSPPL (even one that contains tens of megabytes of data), while still ensuring that your data is never corrupted. In this sense, an NSPPL is analogous to a database. Because of their ability to incrementally store and retrieve data, NSPPLs are particularly well-suited for working with large amounts of data (that is, data that has several elements, that occupies a large number of bytes, or both).\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Overview of Methods\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The methods in NSPPL break down into three general categories:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab Basic methods\
\'b7\tab PPLData methods\
\'b7\tab Performance tuning methods\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 You use the basic methods to perform most NSPPL operations, such as creating an NSPPL, changing its contents, and saving your changes. Basic methods include \b pplWithPath:create:readOnly:\b0 , \b initWithPath:create:readOnly:\b0 , \b rootDictionary\b0 , and \b save\b0 . These are the methods you use the majority of the time.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The PPLData methods provide read-only snapshots of an entire NSPPL as an NSData object. This allows you to pass NSPPLs across processes; passing NSPPLs as binary data is very fast. Wherever a method has the keyword \b ...PPLData\b0  (for example, \b pplWithPath:fromPPLData:readOnly:\b0 ), you use the \b contentsAsData\b0  method to return the contents of an NSPPL as an NSData object. For example:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 NSPPL *originalPPL;   /* Assume this exists. */\
NSString *aPath;      /* Assume this exists. */\
NSPPL *newPPL = [NSPPL pplWithPath:aPath \
    fromPPLData:[originalPPL contentsAsData] readOnly:NO];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The PPLData methods include \b pplWithPath:fromPPLData:readOnly:\b0 , \b initWithPath:fromPPLData:readOnly:\b0 , and \b propertyListWithPPLData:\b0 . These methods provide a fast mechanism for reading and passing read-only copies of an NSPPL. For example, you can have one process continually updating the contents of an NSPPL as multiple read-only copies of the NSPPL are distributed.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The performance tuning methods, \b flush\b0 , \b pushChangesToDisk\b0 ,\b  detachFromFile\b0  and \b setCacheHalfLife:\b0 , let you exercise finer-grained control over the behavior of an NSPPL. You can use \b flush\b0  and \b pushChangesToDisk\b0  in conjunction with \b save\b0  to speed up save operations. \b detachFromFile\b0  ensures that no more changes are written to disk; this is useful if certain error conditions occur such as running out of disk space or if an NSPPL becomes damaged. \b setCacheHalfLife:\b0  lets you fine-tune the trade-offs between memory usage and speed.\
\fs14\
\fs56\
